WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:01.970
Stand by.

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:04.880
We are now entering the
atmosphere of Venus.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:06.240
Attention.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.840
Before the first space programs

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.230
of the 20th century, it was thought

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:14.030
that our closest neighboring
planet may harbor life.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:17.550
I can remember there are images
where there are dinosaurs

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.363
and flying dinosaurs.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:24.800
Thick clouds covering the surface of Venus

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.453
caused humanity's imagination to run wild,

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:30.260
and for good reason.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:33.950
So at first glance, Venus
and Earth are quite similar.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.170
They are about the same size
and about the same mass.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:40.793
And in fact, sometimes Venus
is called our sister planet.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:43.320
But the first probes

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:46.920
set by the Soviet Union
and NASA discovered a world

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:50.100
plagued with a poisonous
greenhouse atmosphere,

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.430
a sweltering surface temperature

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:54.960
and a crushing air pressure.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.620
The Venusian surface has a temperature

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:02.620
of over 450 degrees Celsius,
so lead essentially melts

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:04.220
on the surface of Venus.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.660
It's very hostile.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:06.690
And so attention turned

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.440
to our more hospitable neighbor, Mars,

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.550
leaving Venus largely
neglected for decades

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:13.860
but never forgotten.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:17.720
Scientists long speculated
that microbial life could exist

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.720
in the permanent cloud-layer atmosphere.

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:24.520
It turns out that in the
upper layers of the atmosphere

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:28.280
between 50 to 60 kilometers
above the surface,

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.573
we found conditions which
are not that different

00:01:31.573 --> 00:01:34.163
from the surface condition
we find on Earth.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:38.740
Then in 2020, evidence of biomarkers,

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:42.393
potential signs of life, were
detected in that cloud layer.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.040
It may be the case that
microbial life has adapted,

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.580
has evolved in order to survive
in the high clouds of Venus.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:54.050
And although the claim was later disputed,

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:56.863
it reinvigorated an enthusiasm for Venus.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:00.840
And scientists are
proposing multiple missions

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.010
with a single objective.

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:04.930
We can just pick one thing and go for it.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:08.510
And we can also work on the taboo topic

00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:09.660
of the search for life.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:19.020
The Soviet Union's Venus missions

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.940
gave us the first landings
and images of the surface

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:23.740
of another planet,

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:25.800
the first audio recordings
from the surface

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:26.763
of another planet

00:02:34.387 --> 00:02:36.790
and the first, and so far the only,

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:38.920
planetary balloon missions.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:40.450
Balloons have already been flown

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:43.880
in the Venusian atmosphere,
the Vega I and II balloons.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:46.350
So we know it works.

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:49.590
In 1985, the two spacecraft called Vega I

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:53.450
and Vega II arrived at Venus,
both equipped with landers

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:56.363
and balloons to explore
the cloud-layer atmosphere.

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:00.170
The balloons separated from the crafts

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:03.780
lasted more than 46 hours
while taking measurements.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:06.800
The data from that upper
cloud layer differed greatly

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:08.570
from the hellish surface.

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:13.542
So the winds have speeds of
about 350 or 360 kilometers

00:03:13.542 --> 00:03:15.010
per hour.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:17.980
That's about the speed of a bullet train

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:21.420
and corresponds to the fastest
winds we have ever measured

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:22.253
on Earth.

00:03:22.253 --> 00:03:24.290
If we only look at
temperature and pressure,

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:27.380
the conditions are very similar
to the surface conditions

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:28.213
on Earth.

00:03:28.213 --> 00:03:32.810
However, the clouds, which on
Earth consist of water vapor,

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:35.420
they're actually made of sulfuric acid.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:37.810
So we are looking for
life which can survive

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:39.403
in an extreme environment.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:43.200
The cloud layer

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:46.010
is estimated to be 50 to 100 times drier

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:49.623
than the driest place on Earth,
the Atacama Desert in Chile.

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:55.730
Dr. Armando Azua-Bustos, an astrobiologist

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:58.610
and Atacama native has
spent much of his career

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:03.443
looking at whether life can
exist in extreme environments.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.550
These organisms are called extremophiles,

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:26.630
life that is adapted to
survive in harsh environments,

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:30.047
like a desert with no known water source.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.490
The near-sterile
environment of the Atacama

00:04:56.490 --> 00:05:00.160
is normally used as a testing
bed for finding life on Mars.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.020
But the clouds here could give us clues

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:04.020
about life on Venus too.

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:22.070
But the Venusian
atmosphere isn't just dry.

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:23.933
It's extremely acidic.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:27.690
The Cave of the Lighted House in Mexico

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:30.890
contains toxic amounts of sulfuric acid.

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:32.430
Yet, microbial life has evolved

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:36.170
to use other chemical
processes to obtain energy

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:38.337
other than photosynthesis.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:27.530
While we've been learning a lot

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:29.680
about extremophiles on Earth,

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:31.870
we've also continued to
learn more about Venus

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:33.373
since those early missions.

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:38.310
Now, in terms of knowledge of Venus,

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:41.020
today, we know much more about Venus

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:45.260
such as about the existing
of prebiotic components

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:50.260
such as ammonia and hydrogen cyanide.

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:54.580
We also know that the
greenhouse effect on Venus

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:57.310
started about 1 billion years ago.

00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:00.770
So we know a lot more things about Venus

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:05.320
thanks to many missions
which went to Venus

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:08.163
such as orbital missions
in the past decades.

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:12.420
Very small amounts of oxygen

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:14.000
were detected in the atmosphere

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:17.540
by at least two different
separate independent probes.

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:20.090
And it's amazing because
we also don't know

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:23.100
how oxygen would be made on
Venus at the levels found.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:25.250
And there's sort of enough puzzles

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:28.113
that something really interesting
is happening on Venus.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:31.080
Recent probes to Venus

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:34.150
like the European Space
Agency's Venus Express

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:37.810
and the Japanese Aerospace
Exploration Agency's Akatsuki

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:40.850
has given us a lot of valuable science.

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:42.440
But only Mars has had missions

00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:44.980
specifically looking
for organic compounds,

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:46.940
the ingredients for life.

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:49.093
Then in 2020, this happened.

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:52.540
It's an extraordinary possibility.

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:55.470
Living organisms floating in
the clouds of planet Venus.

00:07:55.470 --> 00:08:00.260
There may be some form of life on Venus.

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:02.310
This gas is called phosphine.

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:04.050
Now, what exactly is it?

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:05.800
Do we have any idea?

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:08.820
A team led by astronomers in the U.K.

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:11.500
reported seeing the spectral
signature of phosphine

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:13.110
in telescope data.

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:15.210
Phosphine's very unusual because on Earth

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:17.230
it's only associated with life,

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:21.700
either bacteria in oxygen-free
environments like wetlands

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:25.810
or made by humans as
pesticides and other chemicals.

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:28.170
Suddenly, there was a
potential biomarker for life

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:29.470
in the Venusian atmosphere.

00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:31.530
And this really changed
a little bit the picture

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:34.633
of astrobiology research
in our solar system.

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:37.870
While some groups have disputed the claim

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:40.190
saying that the phosphine
signal isn't there

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:43.010
or at least at levels less
than previously thought,

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:46.150
it's reinvigorated the interest
in Venus from the public

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:47.730
and scientific community.

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:50.850
So despite all this
controversy of phosphine,

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:54.070
people have started to
relook at the old so-called

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:57.500
legacy data from the
American and Russian probes

00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:59.990
that went into the Venus
atmosphere in the late '70s

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:01.700
and early '80s.

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:06.050
And what's very intriguing is
it looks like there's evidence

00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:07.883
for phosphine from that old data.

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:14.390
One thing everyone can agree on

00:09:14.390 --> 00:09:16.713
is the need for more research into Venus.

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:21.780
NASA and the European Space
Agency recently green lit

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:23.700
three separate missions.

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:27.420
VERITAS will map the planet's
surface and DAVINCI+,

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:30.550
the second NASA mission, will
have an orbiter to image Venus

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:33.070
in the ultraviolet and infrared light,

00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:35.623
and will send a probe
through Venus's atmosphere,

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:39.230
while the European Space Agency's Envision

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:41.240
will take high-resolution radar images

00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:42.623
of the planet's surface.

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:47.100
Although these missions will
give us a lot of valuable data,

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:50.303
none of them are dedicated
solely to finding biomarkers.

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:54.860
In order to really determine
whether there is life,

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:56.290
we need to go there.

00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:58.880
And for us, the question was immediately,

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:02.270
how can we quickly develop
and launch a mission

00:10:02.270 --> 00:10:04.870
in order to determine
whether there's really life

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:07.200
in the Venusian atmosphere or not?

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:11.160
And within two weeks
after the announcement

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:13.460
of the potential discovery of phosphine,

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:16.510
we published a report
on this mission study

00:10:16.510 --> 00:10:20.150
to deploy a fleet of balloons
in the Venusian atmosphere

00:10:20.150 --> 00:10:22.153
and to search for life.

00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:25.540
Balloons are used for scientific studies

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:29.340
on Earth due to their
cheap cost, simple design

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:30.740
and ability to stay in the atmosphere

00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:32.193
for a long period of time.

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:35.740
You can buy high altitude balloons

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:39.850
for maybe a couple of
hundred dollars or euros.

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:43.610
They tend to be fairly robust
so you just launch them

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:46.710
and then you can essentially
forget about them.

00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:50.670
And these are two benefits
which are also applicable

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:52.163
to the Venusian context.

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:55.930
And considering the
technology already exists,

00:10:55.930 --> 00:10:57.380
it could be launched quickly.

00:10:58.350 --> 00:11:03.310
Venusian balloon, which
consists essentially

00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:05.800
of two basic elements,

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:07.970
so one is the balloon sphere

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:10.610
and then we have what we call a ChipSat.

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:14.040
So it's about the size of a credit card.

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:17.910
And we have a very small
payload such as a tiny camera

00:11:17.910 --> 00:11:21.930
or small microscope on
board and all the subsystems

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:25.180
such as power supply and
communications, et cetera.

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:27.370
So far, this proposal hasn't made it

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:29.210
to the hardware phase yet,

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:33.290
but a separate study headed by
Professor Sara Seager at MIT

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:35.720
and funded in part by
Breakthrough Initiatives

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:38.160
is looking at three separate missions,

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:40.650
the first one to launch
via Rocket Lab's rocket

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:41.740
as early as 2023.

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:46.510
We're really, really, absolutely thrilled

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:49.010
that we have a mission to Venus.

00:11:49.010 --> 00:11:51.840
We've partnered with Rocket
Lab to supply the scientist,

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:54.250
science team and science instrument.

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:56.560
And there's room for
like a tiny instrument,

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:00.740
less than 1 kilogram,
that will go on the probe,

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:04.250
and the probe is going to
drop through the atmosphere

00:12:04.250 --> 00:12:05.800
very quickly.

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:08.490
So the probe would last maybe an hour.

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:11.690
And going through the cloud
layers that we care about,

00:12:11.690 --> 00:12:13.930
that will be about three minutes.

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:16.370
So this mission is short,

00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:18.850
and the instrument that the team chose,

00:12:18.850 --> 00:12:20.480
it has a kind of complicated name.

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:23.577
It's called an
autofluorescing nephelometer.

00:12:25.100 --> 00:12:26.890
Autofluorescence refers to the fact

00:12:26.890 --> 00:12:29.360
that we're basically
shining ultraviolet light

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:32.050
on the droplets and looking
for any fluorescence

00:12:32.050 --> 00:12:35.940
that, in other wavelengths,
other colors of light.

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:39.820
And then nephelometer is just
a fancy word for an instrument

00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:43.010
that measures the light that's
scattered from particles.

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:45.510
If you have a black light,
and you're out in the desert

00:12:45.510 --> 00:12:46.980
and you shine it on a scorpion,

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:49.270
the scorpion lights up green colored,

00:12:49.270 --> 00:12:51.850
just like my piece of fluorescent plastic.

00:12:51.850 --> 00:12:55.850
And that demonstrates how organic
compounds, life compounds,

00:12:55.850 --> 00:12:58.730
could be found on Venus with fluorescence.

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:01.070
The instrument, which in its final form

00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:04.150
will fit inside of an estimated
half-kilogram payload,

00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:06.050
will be the first attempt on Venus

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:08.513
to specifically look for biomarkers.

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:12.080
This instrument by itself
is not going to be able

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:16.660
to definitively determine
that there's life on Venus.

00:13:16.660 --> 00:13:20.740
But if we detect organic
compounds in the cloud droplets,

00:13:20.740 --> 00:13:23.063
it's an indication that
there might be life.

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:28.490
If what they find looks promising,

00:13:28.490 --> 00:13:30.220
their next mission would
be a balloon mission

00:13:30.220 --> 00:13:33.483
to look for biomarkers in
different areas of the atmosphere.

00:13:36.390 --> 00:13:38.240
We envision a balloon.

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:40.420
And we have a few very,
very specific things

00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:41.750
we want to measure.

00:13:41.750 --> 00:13:44.940
We'd like to remeasure the
gases that don't belong,

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:48.370
including phosphine
and ammonia and oxygen.

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:50.980
We'd like to check out these
droplets in more detail.

00:13:50.980 --> 00:13:52.840
In particular, we want
to measure the acidity

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:56.560
of the droplets because there's
some new theories out there,

00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:58.230
including one from my team,

00:13:58.230 --> 00:14:00.090
that the droplets aren't all uniform

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:01.850
and that they're not
all incredibly acidic,

00:14:01.850 --> 00:14:05.000
that some might be a more
friendly acidity to life.

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:08.000
And we'd also like to measure more details

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:09.670
about the composition of the droplets,

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:12.763
measure that directly, not
just from light scattering.

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:15.190
And if there are promising discoveries

00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:19.590
from that mission, to ultimately
prove microbes are present,

00:14:19.590 --> 00:14:22.240
they're proposing a third mission.

00:14:22.240 --> 00:14:24.760
We would go to Venus with a balloon

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:26.620
and this balloon would
have a miniature rocket

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:28.130
attached to it.

00:14:28.130 --> 00:14:30.080
And we would collect samples.

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:31.870
So we'd condense the cloud particles,

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:35.360
and we would trap some air
on Venus, some of the gases.

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:39.010
And we would package those,
get it inside the rocket,

00:14:39.010 --> 00:14:41.560
which would launch from the balloon.

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:44.320
And it would intercept with an orbiter

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:46.500
that would then be able
to act as a cruise vehicle

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:49.540
to come home to Earth with
these precious samples,

00:14:49.540 --> 00:14:52.610
so that here on Earth, we could
use our very sophisticated

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:56.920
and exquisite instruments
to look for life.

00:14:56.920 --> 00:14:57.753
These missions

00:14:57.753 --> 00:14:59.610
would be the first balloon missions

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:01.410
to look for life on another planet,

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:02.963
but probably not the last.

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:04.950
There are people who have been trying

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:08.190
to get a balloon mission to
Venus for decades actually.

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:10.160
It's not like we came and had
all these brilliant ideas.

00:15:10.160 --> 00:15:11.810
We're obviously standing on the shoulders

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:14.690
of people before us, you know,
people who are still working

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:15.550
on such missions.

00:15:15.550 --> 00:15:18.340
But with the rise of
private space companies

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:20.780
and the miniaturization of technology,

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:23.100
inexpensive balloon missions like these

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:26.563
could solve mysteries faster
and make space more accessible.

00:15:27.860 --> 00:15:32.860
There's a basic human urge
to find out if we are alone,

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:37.510
but it's also of tremendous
importance if we think about

00:15:37.510 --> 00:15:40.270
if we would find life on Venus,

00:15:40.270 --> 00:15:43.750
did it have the same
origin as life on Earth

00:15:43.750 --> 00:15:46.720
or did it really emerge in parallel?

00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:51.343
And this has huge implications
also for us as humanity.

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:55.790
Every society in human history

00:15:55.790 --> 00:15:59.220
appears to have had astronomy
as part of its culture.

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:01.900
And that is about wanting
to know and explore

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:03.760
what is beyond Earth.

00:16:03.760 --> 00:16:05.690
And today that question,
you know, includes,

00:16:05.690 --> 00:16:06.903
is there life out there?

00:16:08.090 --> 00:16:10.690
I think it's part of wanting
to understand our place

00:16:10.690 --> 00:16:12.220
in the universe.

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:13.053
Are we alone?

00:16:13.053 --> 00:16:14.033
Are we unique?

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:18.983
Or is the universe filled
with other life forms?

